  /// identifier, so it names the relationship rather than the person.
  final String? publisher;

  /// The publisher's own monotonic counter, if they supplied one. Advisory: it orders
  /// the publisher's revisions relative to each other and MUST NOT be trusted to order
  /// them against anything else.
  final int? cardVersion;
  final List<ContactDocumentClaimsItem> claims;

  /// Serialize to a JSON-encodable map, omitting absent members.
  Map<String, dynamic> toJson() => <String, dynamic>{
        if (publisher != null) 'publisher': publisher!,
        if (cardVersion != null) 'cardVersion': cardVersion!,
        'claims': claims.map((e) => e.toJson()).toList(),
      };
}

/// One received version of a contact's document. Revisions exist because an address
/// book that silently replaces a payment address is a phishing surface, and one that
/// says what changed and when is a defence.
class ContactRevision {
  const ContactRevision({
    required this.rev,
    required this.receivedAt,
    this.supersededAt,
    this.document,
  });

  /// Read this payload from a decoded JSON object.
  factory ContactRevision.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) =>
      ContactRevision(
        rev: json['rev'] as int,
        receivedAt: json['receivedAt'] as String,
        supersededAt: json['supersededAt'] as String?,
        document: json['document'] == null
            ? null
            : ContactDocument.fromJson(
                json['document'] as Map<String, dynamic>),
      );

  /// Monotonic per contact, assigned by the recipient. Not the publisher's cardVersion —
  /// a recipient counts what it received, which is the only sequence it can vouch for.
  final int rev;
  final String receivedAt;

  /// When a later revision replaced this one. Null for the current revision.
  final String? supersededAt;
  final ContactDocument? document;

  /// Serialize to a JSON-encodable map, omitting absent members.
  Map<String, dynamic> toJson() => <String, dynamic>{
        'rev': rev,
        'receivedAt': receivedAt,
        if (supersededAt != null) 'supersededAt': supersededAt!,
        if (document != null) 'document': document!.toJson(),
      };
}

/// A colour **name**, resolved by each consumer against its own palette — never a hex
/// value or any other literal. Two reasons, and both are about the consumer rather
/// than the holder. A literal cannot be legible in a terminal, in a light theme and in
/// a dark one at once, so a stored `#8B0000` is a colour that is wrong somewhere and
/// the holder has no way to know where. And a consumer that reserves colours to mean
/// something — an error, a warning, an irreversible act — must be able to keep a
/// holder's decorative choice out of that channel; it cannot do that with an arbitrary
/// value, and it can do it trivially with a closed set it maps itself. The eight
/// members are chosen to be distinguishable from one another and deliberately carry no
/// status connotation: none is named for success, warning or danger.
///
/// An extension type rather than an enum: a value from a newer MINOR of this
/// specification must not crash the parse (SPEC §5.2), and an enum would throw on one.
/// Compare against the constants below, and treat anything else as unrecognised.
extension type const FacetColour(String value) {
  static const FacetColour slate = FacetColour('slate');
  static const FacetColour indigo = FacetColour('indigo');
  static const FacetColour teal = FacetColour('teal');
  static const FacetColour moss = FacetColour('moss');
  static const FacetColour sand = FacetColour('sand');
  static const FacetColour clay = FacetColour('clay');
  static const FacetColour rose = FacetColour('rose');
  static const FacetColour plum = FacetColour('plum');

  /// Every value this specification's schema permits.
  static const List<FacetColour> values = <FacetColour>[
    slate,
    indigo,
    teal,
    moss,
    sand,
    clay,
    rose,
    plum
  ];
}
